# Testing Rules

## Setup
- Jest + React Testing Library
- Test files are co-located: `ComponentName.test.tsx`

## Test Structure

```tsx
import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import UserCard from './UserCard';

describe('UserCard', () => {
  const defaultProps = {
    name: 'John Doe',
    email: 'john@example.com',
  };

  it('should render user name and email', () => {
    render(<UserCard {...defaultProps} />);
    expect(screen.getByText('John Doe')).toBeInTheDocument();
    expect(screen.getByText('john@example.com')).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it('should call onEdit when edit button is clicked', async () => {
    const onEdit = jest.fn();
    render(<UserCard {...defaultProps} onEdit={onEdit} />);
    await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /edit/i }));
    expect(onEdit).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  });
});
```

## Rules
- Test BEHAVIOR, not implementation details
- Prefer `screen.getByRole()` over `screen.getByTestId()`
- Use `userEvent` over `fireEvent` for user interactions
- Wrap components needing Redux/Router/Theme in appropriate providers
- Do NOT test internal state — test what the user sees and does
- One assertion per test when possible
- Name tests descriptively: `it('should display error message when API fails')`
